(a) Application to title 9 provisions. In addition to any other provisions hereinafter contained, the following revocation provisions shall apply to all chapters of title 9 of this Code of Ordinances.
(b) Authority to revoke or suspend licenses for unlawful activities.
(1) All licensees shall operate in compliance with all applicable federal, state, and local laws and ordinances at all times. Failure to maintain such compliance shall constitute grounds for denial, suspension, or revocation of a license.
(2) Notwithstanding any other provision of this chapter, the city may deny, suspend, or revoke a license where the operation of a business is determined to be detrimental to the public health, safety, or welfare, based on documented patterns of unlawful activity, nuisance conditions, or repeated violations.
(3) The city manager for the city is hereby authorized to make investigations, conduct hearings, and suspend or revoke any licenses issued under title 9 of this Code of Ordinances in accordance with the following procedure.
(c) Preliminary investigation; show cause hearing; notice and hearing; findings of fact. Where it is reported to the city manager that any person, firm, corporation or other legal entity holding a license issued under title 9 of this Code of Ordinances ("the licensee") has, with knowledge actual or implied (i.e., where the licensee knew or under the circumstances should have reasonably known), allowed two (2) or more separate unlawful activities to be carried on within or upon the licensee's place of business or business premises, including activities which would constitute violations of the laws of the State of Georgia or any city ordinances, and where such activities would directly or indirectly affect the licensee's qualification to continue to conduct the licensed business, or where such activities involve or affect the manner in which said business was or is being conducted, the licensee shall be notified to appear before the city manager at a time, date and place certain and show cause why the licensee's business license should not be revoked or suspended. For purposes of this section, "separate unlawful activities" shall mean distinct incidents occurring at different times or involving separate acts, regardless of whether citations were issued or criminal charges were filed. The licensee may appear at the hearing in person and may be represented by counsel (at his own expense), and shall have the right to present evidence and cross-examine witnesses. The rules of evidence shall not apply, but the hearing shall be conducted in a fundamentally fair manner. The city manager's decision shall be based upon a preponderance of the evidence. The city manager may consider police reports, incident reports, witness testimony, and other reliable evidence. Within ten (10) business days following the conclusion of the hearing, the city manager shall issue a written order including findings of fact based upon the evidence presented at the hearing, and may:
(1) Find that the evidence does not warrant or authorize revocation or suspension of the license;
(2) Issue a warning to the licensee;
(3) Suspend the license and probate the suspension;
(4) Suspend the license for any period up to sixty (60) days;
(5) Enter an order revoking the license instanter;
(6) Impose reasonable conditions on the continued operation of the business, including but not limited to security measures, modified hours of operation, or compliance plans.
(d) Appeals. Any person aggrieved by a decision of the city manager made pursuant to this section may file a petition for a writ of certiorari in the Superior Court of Clayton County, Georgia in accordance with the laws of the State of Georgia, provided that the decision of the city manager shall not be stayed during the pendency of the proceedings in the superior court, nor shall it be stayed in the event of an appeal from the final order and judgment of the superior court.
(Ord. No. 88-001, 1-18-88; Ord. No. 11-18, § 1, 5-16-2011; Ord. No. 26-06, § 1(Exh. A), 5-4-2026; Ord. No. 26-15, § 1(Exh. A), 8-3-2026)
